{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md | sed -n '35,105p;145,225p;350,570p'\"","aggregated_output":"    35\t**Root discovery is outside this epic (owner decision 3).** A reader holding\n    36\tonly a peer address cannot learn which manifest is current. A restart reads\n    37\tthe local HEAD of the durable-store epic; a remote query is given a manifest\n    38\taddress. Signed root facts and rendezvous are a separate later epic.\n    39\t\n    40\t## 2. Layering and composition\n    41\t\n    42\tThe governing direction, in the owner's words: \"the dht should sit on the raw\n    43\tdatagram layer but expose a dao.stream interface\"; \"Both — A raw datagram\n    44\tlayer exposed as dao.stream, AND a DHT whose own API is dao.stream-shaped,\n    45\tbuilt on it.\"\n    46\t\n    47\tThe DHT is one interpreter, `dao.jing.dht`, between two stream-shaped\n    48\tboundaries. Below it is one raw datagram socket\n    49\t([`dao.stream.datagram.md`](./dao.stream.datagram.md)). Above it is the\n    50\tcontent request medium `dao.jing.content` already defines. It is a pure\n    51\tstepped state machine: no socket, thread, atom, promise, callback, scheduler\n    52\tor clock, on any host.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe composition supplies, as data:\n    55\t\n    56\t| Datum | Meaning |\n    57\t|---|---|\n    58\t| `:local` | A `dao.jing` byte-store handle: this node's own copy of what it holds. |\n    59\t| `:requests` + cursor | Reader on the request stream (section 3). An evicting ring. |\n    60\t| `:answers` | Writer for the exact `:jing/*` answers (section 3). An evicting ring. |\n    61\t| `:facts` | Writer for DHT-qualified facts (section 3). An evicting ring. |\n    62\t| `:ticks` + cursor | Reader on a tick stream (section 5). |\n    63\t| `:traffic` + cursor, `:datagrams` | Reader on the socket's raw traffic stream and its writer handle. **Both absent in solo mode.** |\n    64\t| `:dao.jing.dht/id` | This node's id: 64 lowercase hex characters, 32 bytes of the key space (section 6). |\n    65\t| `:dao.jing.dht/secret` | Root secret bytes for cookies (section 8). Absent in solo mode; required with a socket from S4. |\n    66\t| `:dao.jing.dht/bootstrap` | Contacts `[{:host ip-literal :port p} ...]`: unproven candidates, nothing more. |\n    67\t| `:dao.jing.dht/publish?` | Publication declaration, default `false` (section 4.4). |\n    68\t| `:dao.jing.dht/ack-peers` | Peers a write must be sent to before it is acknowledged. Default 2, floor 2, ceiling `k` (section 4). |\n    69\t| limits and tick budgets | Section 9. |\n    70\t\n    71\t`(dao.jing.dht/state composition) -> state` validates the composition and\n    72\tthrows on a composition defect (an `ack-peers` below 2 or above `k`, a missing\n    73\thandle, and from S4 a socket without a secret). It performs no stream\n    74\toperation.\n    75\t`(dao.jing.dht/step state budget) -> state'` is the only thing that advances\n    76\tthe DHT. One owner per state drives it, at the composition's cadence. The\n    77\tstate is a plain map: handles, cursors, the routing table, and the pending\n    78\ttables.\n    79\t\n    80\t**Solo mode** is a composition with no `:traffic` and no `:datagrams`. It\n    81\topens no socket, holds no pending network work, and answers as section 4.3\n    82\tand 4.5 say. Configuring peers later is a new composition.\n    83\t\n    84\tOne step, in this order, each stage bounded by `budget`:\n    85\t\n    86\t1. Drain `:ticks` to `blocked`; the newest reading is `now`.\n    87\t2. Read `:traffic`: validate each datagram (section 7, 8), deliver replies to\n    88\t   pending queries, serve requests.\n    89\t3. Read `:requests`.\n    90\t4. Advance pending work: expire deadlines against `now`, issue queries,\n    91\t   retries and sends.\n    92\t5. Append answers and facts.\n    93\t\n    94\tA `gap` on `:requests`, `:traffic` or `:ticks` adopts the recovery cursor and\n    95\tappends `{:dao.jing.dht/fact :dao.jing.dht/gap :dao.jing.dht/stream s}`. A\n    96\trequest lost to a gap is never answered; its asker re-asks. Every operation\n    97\there is idempotent by content address, so re-asking is always safe.\n    98\t\n    99\t## 3. The stream API\n   100\t\n   101\t**Requests and answers are the `dao.jing.content` convention**\n   102\t(`src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc`), unchanged and exact. The DHT is one more\n   103\tserving half of that convention, a sibling of `serve-step` whose answers may\n   104\tarrive on a later step. The clients are the existing ones:\n   105\t`dao.jing.content.step` (portable, stepped), `dao.jing.content.driver` (JVM\n   145\t\n   146\t## 4. Writes: local insert, then acknowledged when sent to multiple peers\n   147\t\n   148\tThe owner's contract (2026-09-30), verbatim: \"i want to take the kafka route.\n   149\twrite is acknowledged immediately as long as it is sent out on a network. this\n   150\tworks because the data is written to a peer\"; \"sent to a peer but it must send\n   151\tto multiple peers before it can be considered ack\"; local copy: \"Yes, local +\n   152\tnetwork\". This supersedes the earlier local-verdict acknowledgement.\n   153\t\n   154\t### 4.1 What the acknowledgement asserts\n   155\t\n   156\t**A write is acknowledged when the local insert has succeeded and every\n   157\tdatagram of its store message has been handed to the socket toward at least\n   158\t`ack-peers` distinct peers.**\n   159\t\n   160\t- \"Handed to the socket\" is the raw writer's `append!` answering\n   161\t  `:dao.stream/ok` (`dao.stream.datagram.md` section 4), for every datagram of\n   162\t  the message, chunks included. A peer for which any datagram answered\n   163\t  otherwise is not counted, and the next candidate is tried.\n   164\t- A peer counts only if it is a **proven** routing entry (section 6) that is\n   165\t  **fresh**: a matched reply from it arrived within the last\n   166\t  `cookie-epoch-ticks` by this node's own ticks. That reply is also what\n   167\t  carried the cookie the store uses (section 8). A peer that is not fresh is\n   168\t  pinged first.\n   169\t- The acknowledgement says **sent to N peers**. It never says stored by N\n   170\t  peers, received by N peers, or durable anywhere but locally. The word\n   171\t  \"stored\" appears only in `:dao.jing.dht/confirmed`, and that number is\n   172\t  peers' own replies, which are claims.\n   173\t- `ack-peers` is composition data: default 2, floor 2, ceiling `k`. One peer\n   174\t  is never \"multiple\".\n   175\t- A local collision or an invalid address/bytes pair fails before anything\n   176\t  else, as today.\n   177\t\n   178\t### 4.2 Two entry points, one pending write\n   179\t\n   180\t- **The byte-store handle** (section 5.1). `put-bytes-fn` validates, inserts\n   181\t  into `:local`, appends `{:dao.jing.dht/replicate address}` to the request\n   182\t  stream, and returns the local verdict `:inserted` or `:present` at once.\n   183\t  The return value is the byte-store contract's local verdict. It is **not**\n   184\t  the acknowledgement; the acknowledgement is the `/sent` fact.\n   185\t- **A `:jing/put` request.** The step validates (the one ingress check,\n   186\t  `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`), inserts into `:local`, and holds the answer.\n   187\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result verdict}` is appended when the write is\n   188\t  acknowledged, and in a DHT composition that answer **is** the\n   189\t  acknowledgement.\n   190\t\n   191\tBoth create or join one pending write per address. A pending write records\n   192\tthe address, the request ids waiting on it, the peers sent to, and its\n   193\tdeadline. It never holds the payload; bytes are read from `:local` when sent.\n   194\t\n   195\t### 4.3 How a write proceeds, and what the caller sees when it cannot\n   196\t\n   197\tA pending write never waits on a lookup to acknowledge:\n   198\t\n   199\t1. **Candidates** are the proven routing entries nearest the address's digest\n   200\t   that this node already knows: a table read, no IO.\n   201\t2. **Send** the store message to fresh candidates, nearest first, until `ack-peers` are counted. With a warm table this happens in the\n   202\t   step that read the request: the acknowledgement is immediate.\n   203\t3. **Too few known peers** (a cold bootstrap, a small table): the write starts\n   204\t   an iterative lookup toward the digest and pings candidates; each newly\n   205\t   proven peer is sent to as it appears.\n   206\t4. **Acknowledge** the moment the count reaches `ack-peers`: append the\n   207\t   `/sent` fact and answer every waiting `:jing/put`.\n   208\t5. **Continue after the acknowledgement**, best effort: finish the lookup and\n   209\t   send to the `k` nearest peers not already sent to, so that a reader's\n   210\t   lookup converges on holders. When done or at the deadline, append\n   211\t   `/replicated`. Nothing about the acknowledgement depends on this.\n   212\t\n   213\tIf the count has not reached `ack-peers` when `ack-ticks` have elapsed since\n   214\tthe write began, the write is **not acknowledged**: append the\n   215\t`/unacknowledged` fact and answer every waiting `:jing/put` with\n   216\t`{:jing/request r :jing/unacknowledged reason}`. The pending write is then\n   217\tforgotten. The local copy stays. Trying again is the caller's policy: append\n   218\tthe replicate request again.\n   219\t\n   220\t| Reason | When | Sent anything? |\n   221\t|---|---|---|\n   222\t| `:dao.jing.dht/solo` | No socket is composed. Answered in the step that reads the request. | No |\n   223\t| `:dao.jing.dht/unpublished` | `publish?` is false (section 4.4). Answered at once. | No |\n   224\t| `:dao.jing.dht/oversize` | The encoded store message exceeds `max-message-bytes`. Answered at once. | No |\n   225\t| `:dao.jing.dht/absent` | A replicate request names an address `:local` does not hold. | No |\n   350\t  answered or candidates are exhausted.\n   351\t\n   352\tNAT meeting is not in this epic. The DHT may share a hole-punched socket with\n   353\t`dao.stream.udp` (`dao.stream.datagram.md` section 6), and the identity and\n   354\taddress rules above are what keep wire v1 usable once meeting exists.\n   355\t\n   356\t## 7. The wire (v1)\n   357\t\n   358\tOne CBOR value per datagram, encoded with `dao.stream.cbor` (the stream\n   359\tcodec, canonical profile, available on every host). Not `dao.jing.cbor`, and\n   360\tnot Transit. This is a clean break from today's wire; no fleet exists.\n   361\t\n   362\t```clojure\n   363\t;; request\n   364\t{:dao.jing.dht/v 1\n   365\t :op      :ping | :find | :store | :fetch\n   366\t :q       n                  ; query id: self-minted random safe integer\n   367\t :id      <32 bytes>         ; sender's node id\n   368\t :cookie  <bytes>            ; the cookie this peer last gave the sender; absent on first contact\n   369\t :pad     <byte string>      ; only on a request without a cookie: zero bytes, section 8\n   370\t :target  <32 bytes>         ; :find\n   371\t :address a                  ; :store, :fetch\n   372\t :bytes   <byte string>}     ; :store: the canonical payload bytes\n   373\t\n   374\t;; reply\n   375\t{:dao.jing.dht/v 1\n   376\t :op      :reply\n   377\t :q       n\n   378\t :id      <32 bytes>\n   379\t :cookie  <bytes>            ; a cookie for the requester's observed address\n   380\t ;; then exactly one of:\n   381\t :need-cookie true\n   382\t :ok      boolean            ; :ping, :store\n   383\t :peers   [[<32 bytes> host port] ...]   ; :find\n   384\t :found   boolean :bytes <byte string>}  ; :fetch\n   385\t\n   386\t;; chunk\n   387\t{:dao.jing.dht/v 1\n   388\t :op :chunk :q n :dir :request | :reply\n   389\t :part i :parts k\n   390\t :cookie <bytes>             ; :dir :request only\n   391\t :bytes  <byte string>}\n   392\t```\n   393\t\n   394\t- `:dao.jing.dht/v` is the discriminator on a shared socket and the version.\n   395\t  A datagram that does not decode to a map carrying it is not the DHT's and is\n   396\t  dropped silently. An unknown version is dropped without reply.\n   397\t- Payload and chunk bytes are CBOR byte strings on the wire. Base64 appears\n   398\t  only in stream-visible values (`:jing/bytes`, raw datagram events).\n   399\t- Ids and targets are raw 32-byte strings on the wire and hex in state.\n   400\t- **Peers per `:find` reply: at most 8**, as `[id host port]` tuples. The\n   401\t  iterative lookup asks again for more. S3 proves by test that the largest\n   402\t  legal reply (8 peers, IPv6 literals) encodes within `max-datagram`.\n   403\t- **A reply is matched by `[observed source, :q]`** against a pending query\n   404\t  sent to that address. `:q` alone matches nothing.\n   405\t- **Chunks.** A message whose encoding exceeds the datagram budget is sent as\n   406\t  `:chunk` records, each fitting the budget. Reassembly is keyed\n   407\t  `[observed host, observed port, :dir, :q]`. Duplicates are ignored;\n   408\t  a `:parts` disagreeing with the held count is dropped; accumulated bytes\n   409\t  over `max-message-bytes` drop the partial; incomplete partials are evicted\n   410\t  oldest-first past `max-partial-messages` and by age in ticks. Loss of a part\n   411\t  is loss of the message, recovered by retrying the whole query.\n   412\t- The total encoded size is checked against `max-message-bytes` **before any\n   413\t  chunk is sent**.\n   414\t- The DHT does not use `dao.stream.udp`'s fragment envelope or its\n   415\t  `:dao.stream.remote/id` identity.\n   416\t- **Reliability is the requester's**: each query has a deadline of\n   417\t  `query-ticks` and `tries` attempts, then the peer is unreachable for that\n   418\t  operation.\n   419\t\n   420\t## 8. Hardening\n   421\t\n   422\tImplemented where the DHT reads raw events, never in the raw layer. Non-loopback\n   423\texposure waits for all of it (slice S4).\n   424\t\n   425\t- **Cookie protocol.** Every reply carries a cookie for the requester's\n   426\t  observed address. A requester keeps the newest cookie each peer gave it and\n   427\t  echoes it in every later request and request chunk to that peer. A cookie\n   428\t  is 16 bytes, is a pure function `cookie-for` of the epoch and the observed\n   429\t  host and port, with `epoch = floor(now / cookie-epoch-ticks)`, and is valid\n   430\t  under the current or the previous epoch. Verification recomputes and\n   431\t  compares; it holds no state. The protocol, the gate below and freshness\n   432\t  (4.1) are one mechanism, implemented whole in S2.\n   433\t- **`cookie-for` has two definitions, and only it differs between slices.**\n   434\t  - *S2 stand-in:* the first 16 bytes of `SHA-256(epoch | observed-host |\n   435\t    observed-port)`. Unkeyed and deterministic, so tests can predict it, and\n   436\t    forgeable, which is why exposure stays loopback-only.\n   437\t  - *S4 replacement:* the first 16 bytes of `MAC(epoch-secret, observed-host |\n   438\t    observed-port)` with `epoch-secret = MAC(:dao.jing.dht/secret, epoch)`.\n   439\t    The MAC is HMAC-SHA-256 from each host's library; nothing is\n   440\t    hand-rolled. From S4 a socket composition without a secret is a\n   441\t    composition defect.\n   442\t  The wire, the state and every other rule are identical under both.\n   443\t- **The gate.** A request or request chunk without a valid cookie causes no\n   444\t  work: no lookup, no storage, no reassembly, no routing entry. The only\n   445\t  possible response is the **need-cookie reply**, exactly\n   446\t  `{:dao.jing.dht/v 1 :op :reply :q n :id <32 bytes> :cookie <16 bytes>\n   447\t  :need-cookie true}` and nothing else.\n   448\t- **No amplification, without exception.** The need-cookie reply is sent only\n   449\t  if its encoded length is less than or equal to the length of the datagram\n   450\t  that caused it. Otherwise the datagram is dropped in **silence**. There is\n   451\t  no other reply to an unproven source.\n   452\t- **First contact is padded.** A requester sending without a cookie adds\n   453\t  `:pad`, a zero-filled byte string, so that the datagram is at least\n   454\t  `first-contact-bytes` long. `first-contact-bytes` is 256, a constant of\n   455\t  wire v1. S2 proves by test that the need-cookie reply never encodes to more\n   456\t  than 256 bytes. A request carrying a cookie carries no `:pad`. A request\n   457\t  without a cookie is only ever a padded `:ping`: a requester never sends\n   458\t  `:find`, `:store`, `:fetch` or a chunk to a peer it holds no cookie for.\n   459\t- **Recovery.** On a need-cookie reply the requester stores the new cookie\n   460\t  and re-sends the query once; that re-send does not consume a try. A query\n   461\t  that times out against a peer may have been dropped in silence (an expired\n   462\t  cookie on a small datagram), so the requester discards that peer's cookie\n   463\t  and sends a padded `:ping` before the next try.\n   464\t- **A request chunk is verified before any partial state is allocated**, and\n   465\t  an invalid one falls under the gate and the size rule above like any other\n   466\t  datagram.\n   467\t- **Reply chunks** are accepted only for a pending query to that observed\n   468\t  address; the state was allocated by this node's own request.\n   469\t- **Inbound `:store`** re-verifies address against bytes and canonicality\n   470\t  before writing, and is refused (`:ok false`) when `publish?` is false or the\n   471\t  inbound storage bound is reached. Refusal is explicit, never silence.\n   472\t- **Bounds**, all composition data (section 9): pending queries, pending\n   473\t  writes, pending gets, partial messages and bytes, globally and per observed\n   474\t  source; work per step.\n   475\t\n   476\t## 9. Limits and defaults\n   477\t\n   478\t| Datum | Default | Notes |\n   479\t|---|---|---|\n   480\t| `:dao.jing.dht/ack-peers` | 2 | Floor 2, ceiling `k`. |\n   481\t| `:dao.jing.dht/ack-ticks` | 5000 | Acknowledgement deadline per pending write. |\n   482\t| `:dao.jing.dht/get-ticks` | 5000 | Deadline per pending get. |\n   483\t| `:dao.jing.dht/query-ticks`, `/tries` | 500, 3 | Per query, today's figures. |\n   484\t| `:dao.jing.dht/max-message-bytes` | 65536 | Encoded message bound, send and reassembly. S3 measures real covered-index nodes at the default branching factor and confirms or raises this default before S4; it is composition data, not a wire constant. |\n   485\t| `:dao.jing.dht/max-partial-messages` | 64 | Global; per-source share is a quarter. |\n   486\t| `:dao.jing.dht/max-pending-writes`, `/max-pending-gets` | 64, 64 | Beyond: `/busy`, or not found with a `/miss`. |\n   487\t| `:dao.jing.dht/cookie-epoch-ticks` | 60000 | |\n   488\t| `:dao.jing.dht/max-inbound-bytes` | composition's | Inbound `:store` bound; S4. |\n   489\t| datagram budget | 1200 | The socket's `:max-bytes`. |\n   490\t\n   491\tEvery stream the DHT is composed over is an evicting ring: requests,\n   492\tanswers, facts, raw traffic. None is a complete-history log.\n   493\t\n   494\t## 10. Today's code, and the slices that replace it\n   495\t\n   496\tWhat the tree holds on 2026-09-30, all of it replaced by this epic with no\n   497\tcompatibility path:\n   498\t\n   499\t| Today | Fate |\n   500\t|---|---|\n   501\t| `dao.jing.dht/IDhtNet`, `lookup`, `create-content-dht` (a waiting transport protocol; put replicates and get fetches synchronously) | Deleted in S2. |\n   502\t| `dao.jing.dht.node` (`create-node`, `create-content-dht-udp`; JVM only, own `DatagramSocket` and receiver thread, one Transit-JSON map per datagram, node id `sha256(host:port)`, claimed `:from` observed unvalidated, replies matched by id alone, oversize dropped silently) | Deleted in S3. |\n   503\t| `dao.jing.dht.kad` | Kept. |\n   504\t| `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc`, `test/dao/jing/dht/node_test.cljc`, the fake net in `test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc` | Rewritten in S2 and S3. |\n   505\t\n   506\t### Slice plan\n   507\t\n   508\tOrder: **S1 ∥ S2 → S3 → S4 → S5.** S1 and S2 own disjoint files and neither\n   509\tconsumes anything the other produces. No slice before S5 touches\n   510\t`yin/repl/*`.\n   511\t\n   512\t**Base64 seam.** The format is one: padded standard-alphabet Base64, strict.\n   513\tS2's codec is the existing `dao.jing/bytes->base64` and\n   514\t`dao.jing/base64->bytes`, frozen as its seam; S2 adds no codec and waits on\n   515\tnone. S1 creates `dao.stream.base64` for the datagram layer, byte-for-byte\n   516\tthe same format, and does not touch `dao.jing`. S3, which joins the two,\n   517\trepoints `dao.jing`'s two functions onto `dao.stream.base64` and proves both\n   518\tagree on every test vector.\n   519\t\n   520\t**S1 — raw datagram stream.** `dao.stream.datagram` (portable), the three\n   521\thost seams, `dao.stream.base64`, `dao.stream.udp`'s `port-step!`.\n   522\tAcceptance:\n   523\t- Exact byte and observed-source round trips over loopback on JVM, Node and\n   524\t  Dart, through the writer handle and the traffic ring.\n   525\t- `bound`, `bind-failed`, `send-failed` and `closed` are observed as events;\n   526\t  no seam takes a function to invoke.\n   527\t- Hostname destination, bad Base64 and oversize are `invalid-value` with no\n   528\t  send; a closed socket is `closed`; inbound oversize is never deposited.\n   529\t- A slow reader observes `gap` on the traffic ring.\n   530\t- `test/dao/stream/udp_test.cljc` and every `dao.stream.remote` test pass\n   531\t  unchanged; host seam tests are rewritten.\n   532\t- Value-channel throughput is measured before and after and reported.\n   533\t\n   534\t**S2 — DHT core on in-memory sockets.** `dao.jing.dht` state and step,\n   535\tlookup as state, pending writes and gets, the store handle, solo mode, the\n   536\tJVM facade, the `:jing/unacknowledged` completion in `dao.jing.content.step`.\n   537\tPeers are wired through ring buffers carrying raw datagram values; S2 needs\n   538\tno socket and no S1. It speaks the section 7 wire for single-datagram\n   539\tmessages and implements the whole cookie protocol of section 8 (issue, echo,\n   540\tverify, the gate, the size rule, padding, freshness) with the S2 stand-in\n   541\t`cookie-for`. Chunks are S3; the keyed MAC is S4. Acceptance:\n   542\t- An acknowledged write used, for every counted peer, the cookie that peer's\n   543\t  fresh reply carried; a store with a missing or wrong cookie causes no work.\n   544\t- The need-cookie reply never encodes to more than `first-contact-bytes`, and\n   545\t  a cookie-less datagram shorter than that reply is answered with silence.\n   546\t- Requests and answers are the exact `:jing/*` shapes; the unmodified content\n   547\t  clients drive a DHT.\n   548\t- With a warm table a write is acknowledged in the step that read it, after\n   549\t  exactly `ack-peers` counted sends, and the fact says `/sent`.\n   550\t- `put-bytes-fn` performs no lookup and no peer wait; the publish/readback\n   551\t  round completes against `:local`.\n   552\t- Each `/unacknowledged` reason of 4.3 is produced by a test, including cold\n   553\t  bootstrap reaching `too-few-peers` at the tick deadline and then\n   554\t  succeeding once peers are proven.\n   555\t- Solo mode composes no socket streams and holds no pending work.\n   556\t- A file-backed local verdict survives restart.\n   557\t- Time advances only by appended ticks; no test sleeps.\n   558\t- The JVM facade has one step owner and respects deadlines.\n   559\t- `dao.jing.md`'s backend list and status are updated.\n   560\t\n   561\t**S3 — real sockets and chunks.** Depends on S1 and S2. Chunk records and\n   562\tthe move onto `dao.stream.datagram` sockets. Loopback only. Acceptance:\n   563\t- Two and three peers exchange small queries and multi-datagram segments\n   564\t  under injected loss and reordering.\n   565\t- The largest legal `:find` reply fits one datagram.\n   566\t- Oversize refuses before any send; malformed, duplicate and inconsistent\n   567\t  chunks stay within every bound.\n   568\t- A chunked write counts a peer only when every chunk answered `ok`.\n   569\t- **One transport-neutral chunk split/absorb utility is used by both\n   570\t  `dao.stream.udp` and the DHT**, each keeping its own wire fields and keys.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
