{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.datagram.md | sed -n '60,210p'\"","aggregated_output":"    60\t\n    61\t- `:dao.stream.datagram/bytes` is padded standard-alphabet Base64 of the\n    62\t  exact datagram payload: no whitespace, no URL-safe alphabet. A zero-length\n    63\t  datagram is the empty string. Host byte arrays never cross onto a stream\n    64\t  (`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries); Base64 is what lets a traffic stream be\n    65\t  served over any channel.\n    66\t- `:dao.stream.datagram/host` is always an **IP literal** in its textual form,\n    67\t  IPv4 or IPv6. A source host is the address the socket observed. A\n    68\t  destination host that is not an IP literal is `invalid-value`: name\n    69\t  resolution can wait, and no operation waits. Resolution is a separate\n    70\t  interpreter or host policy that produces literals.\n    71\t- `:dao.stream.datagram/port` is an integer in 1..65535.\n    72\t- A datagram event carries `:dao.stream.datagram/bytes` and\n    73\t  `:dao.stream.datagram/source`; a lifecycle event carries\n    74\t  `:dao.stream.datagram/event`. A value carrying neither is not this layer's.\n    75\t\n    76\tThe strict Base64 codec lives at or below `dao.stream` (one portable helper\n    77\tnamespace, `dao.stream.base64`, created in S1). This layer never requires\n    78\t`dao.jing`: a boundary depends on nothing above it. `dao.jing` keeps its own\n    79\tidentical-format functions until S3 repoints them onto this helper\n    80\t(`dao.jing.dht.md` section 10, Base64 seam).\n    81\t\n    82\t## 3. The socket, its identity and its descriptor\n    83\t\n    84\tBinding is a host act, not `create!`. On Node and Dart the bound port of an\n    85\tephemeral bind is not knowable until the host answers, so identity and\n    86\treachability cannot both be settled at once, which `dao.stream.md` (Creation\n    87\tand Attachment) requires of `create!`. The composition therefore proceeds in\n    88\ttwo explicit moves.\n    89\t\n    90\t1. **Bind.** The host seam (`dao.stream.datagram.jvm`, `.node`, `.dart`)\n    91\t   takes `{:identity id :deposit <writer> :bind-host h :bind-port p\n    92\t   :max-bytes n}` and returns at once `{:send! f :close! f}`. `id` is a\n    93\t   string the composition mints. `:bind-host` is the local interface address\n    94\t   (the wildcard address is legal); `:bind-port` 0 asks for an ephemeral\n    95\t   port. The seam takes the deposit writer and nothing else from above: it is\n    96\t   handed no function to invoke.\n    97\t2. **Observe `bound`, then compose the writer.** The seam deposits\n    98\t   `:dao.stream.datagram/bound` with the actual local address, on every host,\n    99\t   synchronous bind or not. The composition reads that event and calls the\n   100\t   portable constructor `dao.stream.datagram/writer` with the seam and the\n   101\t   complete descriptor. A failed bind deposits `:bind-failed` and there is no\n   102\t   writer.\n   103\t\n   104\tThe descriptor:\n   105\t\n   106\t```clojure\n   107\t{:dao.stream/type                :dao.stream/datagram\n   108\t :dao.stream/identity            id\n   109\t :dao.stream.datagram/bind-host  h     ; the LOCAL interface this socket is bound on\n   110\t :dao.stream.datagram/bind-port  p}    ; the LOCAL port actually bound, never 0\n   111\t```\n   112\t\n   113\t- The keys say `bind-` because they name the **local** socket. The UDP value\n   114\t  channel's descriptor keys, `:dao.stream.udp/host` and `:dao.stream.udp/port`\n   115\t  (`dao.stream.remote.md` 3.2), name the **remote destination** its writer\n   116\t  sends toward. The two descriptors appear side by side in one composition and\n   117\t  must never be read as the same kind of address.\n   118\t- A bind address is not a reachable address. Behind a NAT the address peers\n   119\t  observe differs from both keys; nothing here claims otherwise, and no\n   120\t  consumer advertises `bind-host`/`bind-port` as where it can be reached.\n   121\t- `attach!` on this descriptor off its host is `:dao.stream/not-found`, as a\n   122\t  ring buffer's is. On its own host it resolves only against sockets a\n   123\t  composition chose to keep in its host-composed attach closure; there is no\n   124\t  registry. There is no `:dao.stream/create` entry.\n   125\t\n   126\t## 4. The writer handle\n   127\t\n   128\tDeclared surface: `#{:writer :closable}`. `descriptor` answers `ok` with the\n   129\tdescriptor above and its identity, before and after close.\n   130\t\n   131\t`append!` outcomes:\n   132\t\n   133\t| Outcome | When |\n   134\t|---|---|\n   135\t| `:dao.stream/ok` | The datagram was handed to the socket. It asserts nothing about the wire, the path, or the destination. |\n   136\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value` | Not an outbound value of section 2: a malformed destination, a host that is not an IP literal, bytes that are not strict Base64, or a decoded length over `:max-bytes`. Nothing was sent. |\n   137\t| `:dao.stream/closed` | The socket was closed. Nothing was sent. |\n   138\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The host send failed synchronously. Failures are clean: nothing was sent. |\n   139\t\n   140\tExcluded outcomes, with reasons: `full`, because this layer holds no outbound\n   141\tqueue and a socket under pressure loses datagrams, which is the medium's own\n   142\tdeclared nature, not a refusal; `refused`, because no policy is composed in\n   143\tthis layer (a gate composed over the handle may produce it,\n   144\t`dao.stream.middleware.md`).\n   145\t\n   146\tA host whose send reports failure only later (Node, Dart) deposits\n   147\t`:dao.stream.datagram/send-failed` on the traffic stream; that is the channel\n   148\tthis transport declares for an answer it cannot give at call time\n   149\t(`dao.stream.md`, Writing).\n   150\t\n   151\t`close!` answers `ok`, closes the host socket, and is idempotent. The seam\n   152\tdeposits `:dao.stream.datagram/closed` when it still can; the irreducible\n   153\tcorner of `dao.stream.md` (Surfaces, observably gone) applies when it cannot.\n   154\t\n   155\t`:max-bytes` is composition data, default 1200, and is symmetric: an inbound\n   156\tdatagram longer than it is dropped below the transform as protocol\n   157\tvalidation, counted and never deposited. 1200 clears the IPv6 minimum path\n   158\tMTU with headers to spare.\n   159\t\n   160\t## 5. The traffic stream\n   161\t\n   162\t- The deposit destination is supplied by the composition and **must be an\n   163\t  evicting ring** (`dao.stream.ringbuffer`). A complete-history log under a\n   164\t  network-facing socket is a host assembly defect: any sender on the network\n   165\t  could grow it without bound.\n   166\t- A ring never refuses, so the deposit-admission rule of `dao.stream.md`\n   167\t  (Surfaces) holds by construction.\n   168\t- A `:dao.stream/gap` on the traffic stream means datagrams were lost to this\n   169\t  reader. It is indistinguishable from network loss and is handled the same\n   170\t  way, by whatever loss policy the reading interpreter already has.\n   171\t- Capacity is counted in events. One event is at most `:max-bytes` of payload\n   172\t  as Base64, so memory is bounded by `capacity * ceil(max-bytes * 4 / 3)` plus\n   173\t  envelope.\n   174\t- The seam's transform deposits and returns. It judges no datagram and\n   175\t  invokes nothing above itself.\n   176\t\n   177\t## 6. Peer observers on one socket\n   178\t\n   179\tAny number of interpreters may read one traffic stream, each with its own\n   180\tcursor, and may write through the one writer handle. None knows the others\n   181\texist. A datagram that is not an interpreter's own is dropped by that\n   182\tinterpreter without reply and without diagnostics on any shared stream.\n   183\t\n   184\tTwo interpreters are specified today and may share a socket, which is what\n   185\tlets a hole-punched mapping (`dao.stream.remote.md` section 4) serve both:\n   186\t\n   187\t- `dao.jing.dht` owns exactly the datagrams whose decoded value is a map\n   188\t  carrying `:dao.jing.dht/v` (`dao.jing.dht.md`, The wire).\n   189\t- `dao.stream.udp` owns every other decodable value, and drops a map carrying\n   190\t  `:dao.jing.dht/v` before its deposit.\n   191\t\n   192\tA further interpreter on a shared socket declares its own discriminator here.\n   193\t\n   194\t## 7. `dao.stream.udp` rebuilt on this layer\n   195\t\n   196\t`dao.stream.udp` keeps its public surface: the descriptor, `make-port`,\n   197\t`send-value!`, `send-to!`, `receive!`, `projection`, `step!`,\n   198\t`make-attacher`, the fragment envelope, and both reassembly bounds. What\n   199\tchanges is what feeds it.\n   200\t\n   201\t- `make-port`'s `:send!` is composed as a closure that encodes the bytes as\n   202\t  Base64 and appends one outbound value to the raw writer. `send-value!`\n   203\t  answers the raw writer's first non-`ok` outcome instead of discarding it.\n   204\t- **`port-step!`** is new: one step that reads the raw traffic stream from\n   205\t  the port's own raw cursor to `blocked`, bounded by a budget, skips lifecycle\n   206\t  events and `:dao.jing.dht/v` maps, decodes each datagram event's Base64, and\n   207\t  calls `receive!` with the source address and bytes. A raw `gap` adopts the\n   208\t  recovery cursor: those datagrams are lost, and the link's resend rule\n   209\t  recovers exactly as it does for network loss.\n   210\t- One owner per port drives `port-step!`, and the composition drives it\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
